→0
Q13:∆Q13
→0
Q14:∆Q14
→0
NOTYET:'QUIZ ';∆N;' IS NOT YET WRITTEN.'
→0
BAD:'THERE ARE 16 LESSONS. TYPE CONTENTS TO SEE THEM.'
∇
∇R←W ∆IS A
⍝ IS THE ANSWER A THE WORD W? AT A QUAD PROMPT THE WORDS GO AND
⍝ STOP ARE THE VARIABLES OF THOSE NAMES, WHICH HOLD THEMSELVES.
R←((⍴,W)=⍴,A)∧∧/(,W)=(⍴,W)↑,A
∇
∇∆TRY
'(TRY IT. TYPE GO TO GO ON, OR STOP TO LEAVE.)'
∇
∇R←∆PAUSE;∆A
⍝ THE PAUSE BETWEEN SECTIONS. WHAT THE READER TYPES IS READ WITH
⍝ QUAD AND PRINTED, UNTIL GO OR STOP. R IS 1 FOR STOP.
L:∆A←⎕
R←'STOP' ∆IS ∆A
→R/0
→('GO' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆A
→L
∇
∇R←∆READ
⍝ READ A QUIZ ANSWER AS APL. THE QUESTION IS PRINTED BY THE CALLER.
R←⎕
∇
∇R←W ∆CHECK A
⍝ IS THE ANSWER A THE WANTED VALUE W? SAME COUNT, SAME VALUES,
⍝ AND THE SAME RANK; A SCALAR FOR A ONE-ELEMENT VECTOR PASSES
⍝ WITH A REMARK. R IS 1 FOR RIGHT.
R←0
→((×/⍴,W)≠×/⍴,A)/WRONG
→(~∧/,(,W)=,A)/WRONG
→((⍴⍴W)=⍴⍴A)/RIGHT
'RIGHT VALUE, THOUGH ONE IS A SCALAR AND ONE A VECTOR.'
R←1
→0
RIGHT:'RIGHT.'
R←1
→0
WRONG:'NO. THE ANSWER IS:'
W
∇
∇∆L1 ∆F;∆Q
→(S1,S2,S3,S4,S5)[1⌈5⌊∆F]
S1:'LESSON 1: WHERE YOU ARE'
''
'THE SIX SPACES YOU TYPE AFTER ARE THE PROMPT. APL WAITS'
'THERE. WHAT YOU TYPE IS INDENTED; WHAT APL ANSWERS STARTS'
'AT THE LEFT MARGIN. TRY:'
''
'      2+2'
'      3×4'
''
'IT ANSWERS AT ONCE. THERE IS NO PROGRAM TO WRITE FIRST.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S2:'A LINE THAT BEGINS WITH A RIGHT PARENTHESIS IS NOT APL BUT'
'A SYSTEM COMMAND: A WORD TO THE SYSTEM AROUND APL. TWO OF'
'THEM SAY WHERE YOU ARE. TRY:'
''
'      )DIALECT'
'      )WSID'
''
'THE FIRST ANSWERS (A) ''70 OR (B) ''75. SW-APL HAS TWO MODES,'
'ONE MODELLED ON THE APL OF 1970 AND ONE ON THE APL OF 1975.'
'THIS COURSE RUNS IN BOTH; LESSON 16 SAYS HOW THEY DIFFER.'
'THE SECOND ANSWERS COURSE, THE NAME OF THIS WORKSPACE: THE'
'FUNCTIONS AND VARIABLES YOU ARE WORKING WITH.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S3:'WORKSPACES ARE KEPT IN NUMBERED LIBRARIES. TRY:'
''
'      )LIBS'
'      )LIB 2'
''
'LIBRARY 0 IS YOURS, WHERE )SAVE WRITES. LIBRARY 1 IS WHAT'
'SW-APL SHIPS. LIBRARY 2 IS THIS COURSE AND ITS COMPANIONS.'
')LIB N LISTS A LIBRARY AND )LOAD N NAME LOADS ONE, WHICH IS'
'HOW YOU GOT HERE. LESSON 15 IS ABOUT WORKSPACES.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S4:'WHEN YOU DO NOT KNOW WHAT TO TYPE, ASK. TRY:'
''
'      )HELP'
'      )HELP LIBS'
''
'THE FIRST LISTS THE SYSTEM COMMANDS, AND SAYS WHICH ARE'
'SW-APL''S OWN AND WHICH ARE HISTORICAL. THE SECOND EXPLAINS'
'ONE. )HELP, )LIBS AND )DIALECT ARE SW-APL''S; THE REST IS'
'WHAT THE ORIGINAL SYSTEMS HAD.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S5:'THIS IS HOW A LESSON WORKS: A FEW LINES, SOMETHING TO TRY,'
'AND A PAUSE. AT THE PAUSE THE PROMPT IS A QUAD AND A COLON,'
'AND ANY EXPRESSION OR SYSTEM COMMAND YOU TYPE IS RUN AND'
'ITS ANSWER PRINTED. GO GOES ON, STOP LEAVES. A MISTAKE IS'
'REPORTED AND THE PROMPT COMES BACK, SO TRY FREELY.'
''
'NEXT TAKES YOU TO THE LESSON AFTER THE LAST ONE YOU READ,'
'CONTENTS LISTS THEM ALL, LESSON N GOES TO ANY ONE, AND'
'LESSON N S TO ITS SECTION S.'
'EACH LESSON ENDS WITH A QUIZ: A QUESTION, THE QUAD PROMPT,'
'AND YOUR ANSWER AS APL, A NUMBER OR AN EXPRESSION THAT'
'MAKES ONE. STOP LEAVES A QUIZ TOO.'
''
'END OF LESSON 1. TYPE QUIZ 1 TO CHECK, OR NEXT TO GO ON.'
∇
∇∆Q1;∆S;∆A
∆S←0
'WHAT DOES 2+2 GIVE? TYPE THE NUMBER.'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+4 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHICH LIBRARY IS YOURS, WHERE )SAVE WRITES?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+0 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHICH LIBRARY HOLDS THIS COURSE?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+2 ∆CHECK ∆A
'HOW MANY LESSONS ARE THERE?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+16 ∆CHECK ∆A
'YOU GOT ';∆S;' OF 4.'
∇
∇∆L2 ∆F;∆Q
→(S1,S2,S3,S4)[1⌈4⌊∆F]
S1:'LESSON 2: NUMBERS'
''
'THE FOUR ARITHMETIC FUNCTIONS ARE + - × AND ÷. TRY:'
''
'      7÷2'
'      2×3+4'
'      (2×3)+4'
''
'DIVISION GIVES 3.5, NOT 3: A NUMBER IS A NUMBER. AND 2×3+4'
'IS 14, NOT 10. APL READS A LINE FROM THE RIGHT: 3+4 FIRST,'
'THEN 2× THAT. THERE IS NO PRECEDENCE AMONG FUNCTIONS; ONLY'
'PARENTHESES CHANGE THE ORDER.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S2:'A NEGATIVE NUMBER IS WRITTEN WITH A HIGH MINUS, ¯, WHICH IS'
'PART OF THE NUMBER LIKE A DIGIT. THE MINUS SIGN, -, IS A'
'FUNCTION. TRY:'
''
'      ¯3+5'
'      2-3'
'      -5'
''
'EVERY ARITHMETIC FUNCTION HAS A ONE-ARGUMENT FORM TOO:'
'-5 IS NEGATIVE FIVE, ÷4 IS ONE QUARTER, ×¯7 0 7 IS THE'
'SIGN OF EACH, AND |¯7 IS THE MAGNITUDE, 7.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S3:'⌈ AND ⌊ ARE MAXIMUM AND MINIMUM WITH TWO ARGUMENTS, AND'
'CEILING AND FLOOR WITH ONE. TRY:'
''
'      3⌈5'
'      ⌈2.4'
'      ⌊2.4'
''
'| WITH TWO ARGUMENTS IS THE REMAINDER, THE LEFT ONE BEING'
'THE DIVISOR: 3|17 IS 2.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S4:'* IS POWER, AND ALONE IT IS E TO THE POWER: *1 IS'
'2.718281828. ⍟ IS LOGARITHM, NATURAL ALONE AND TO A BASE'
'WITH TWO: 10⍟100 IS 2. ○1 IS PI, AND 1○X IS THE SINE OF X.'
'! IS FACTORIAL, AND 2!5 IS THE NUMBER OF WAYS TO CHOOSE 2'
'FROM 5, WHICH IS 10. TRY:'
''
'      2*10'
'      2*0.5'
'      !5'
''
'END OF LESSON 2. TYPE QUIZ 2 TO CHECK, OR NEXT TO GO ON.'
∇
∇∆Q2;∆S;∆A
∆S←0
'WHAT IS 2×3+4?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+14 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS ¯3+5?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+2 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS 3|17, THE REMAINDER OF 17 DIVIDED BY 3?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+2 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS ⌈2.4?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+3 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS 2*10?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+1024 ∆CHECK ∆A
'YOU GOT ';∆S;' OF 5.'
∇
∇∆L3 ∆F;∆Q
→(S1,S2,S3,S4)[1⌈4⌊∆F]
S1:'LESSON 3: VECTORS'
''
'NUMBERS TYPED IN A ROW MAKE A VECTOR, AND A FUNCTION WORKS'
'ON EVERY ELEMENT AT ONCE. TRY:'
''
'      1 2 3+4 5 6'
'      1 2 3×2'
''
'THE FIRST ADDS ELEMENT TO ELEMENT. THE SECOND MULTIPLIES'
'EVERY ELEMENT BY 2: A SINGLE NUMBER STRETCHES TO FIT.'
'TWO VECTORS OF DIFFERENT LENGTHS DO NOT FIT, AND THAT IS A'
'LENGTH ERROR.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S2:'⍳N IS THE FIRST N COUNTING NUMBERS, AND ⍴V IS HOW MANY'
'ELEMENTS V HAS. TRY:'
''
'      ⍳5'
'      2×⍳5'
'      ⍴3 4 5 6 7'
''
'⍳ IS THE INDEX GENERATOR AND ⍴ IS RHO, THE SHAPE. MUCH OF'
'APL IS BUILT FROM THESE TWO.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S3:'WITH TWO ARGUMENTS ⍴ IS RESHAPE: N⍴V IS N ELEMENTS TAKEN'
'FROM V, CYCLING ROUND WHEN V RUNS OUT. THE COMMA JOINS TWO'
'VECTORS. TRY:'
''
'      5⍴1 2'
'      2⍴7'
'      1 2,3 4'
''
'A COMMA ALONE MAKES A VECTOR OF ANYTHING: ,7 IS A VECTOR'
'OF ONE ELEMENT, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS THE NUMBER 7.'
'⍴7 IS EMPTY, SINCE A LONE NUMBER HAS NO LENGTH, BUT ⍴,7'
'IS 1.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S4:'A VECTOR MAY HAVE NO ELEMENTS AT ALL. ⍳0 IS ONE, AND SO IS'
'0⍴5. IT PRINTS AS AN EMPTY LINE. TRY:'
''
'      ⍳0'
'      ⍴⍳0'
''
'EMPTY VECTORS ARE NOT A CURIOSITY: A FUNCTION THAT WORKS'
'ON A VECTOR OF ANY LENGTH WORKS ON ONE OF LENGTH ZERO,'
'AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THE END OF A LIST NO SPECIAL CASE.'
''
'END OF LESSON 3. TYPE QUIZ 3 TO CHECK, OR NEXT TO GO ON.'
∇
∇∆Q3;∆S;∆A
∆S←0
'WHAT IS ⍳4?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+1 2 3 4 ∆CHECK ∆A
'HOW MANY ELEMENTS HAS 3 4 5 6 7? TYPE THE NUMBER.'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+5 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS 2 3 4×2?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+4 6 8 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS 5⍴1 2?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+1 2 1 2 1 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS 1 2,3 4?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+1 2 3 4 ∆CHECK ∆A
'YOU GOT ';∆S;' OF 5.'
∇
∇∆L4 ∆F;∆Q
→(S1,S2,S3,S4)[1⌈4⌊∆F]
S1:'LESSON 4: COMPARISON AND LOGIC'
''
'< ≤ = ≥ > AND ≠ COMPARE, AND ANSWER 1 FOR TRUE AND 0 FOR'
'FALSE. ON A VECTOR THEY ANSWER FOR EVERY ELEMENT. TRY:'
''
'      3<5'
'      1 2 3=2'
'      1 2 3>2'
''
'CHARACTERS COMPARE TOO: ''ABC''=''B'' IS 0 1 0.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S2:'∧ IS AND, ∨ IS OR, AND ~ IS NOT. THEY WORK ON 1 AND 0. TRY:'
''
'      1∧0'
'      1∨0'
'      ~1 0 1'
''
'≠ ON 1 AND 0 IS EXCLUSIVE OR: 1≠1 0 IS 0 1. ⍲ AND ⍱ ARE'
'NAND AND NOR, THE TWO NEGATED.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S3:'1 AND 0 ARE NUMBERS, NOT A SEPARATE KIND OF VALUE. SO A'
'COMPARISON CAN BE USED IN ARITHMETIC. TRY:'
''
'      2×1 2 3>1'
'      (1 2 3>1)×10 20 30'
''
'MULTIPLYING BY A COMPARISON KEEPS THE ELEMENTS THAT PASS'
'AND ZEROES THE REST. THE NEXT LESSON COUNTS THEM.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S4:'⌈ AND ⌊ FROM LESSON 2 ARE COMPARISONS OF A KIND: 3⌈5 IS'
'THE LARGER. ON 1 AND 0, ⌈ IS OR AND ⌊ IS AND. TRY:'
''
'      1⌈0'
'      1⌊0'
'      3 1 4⌈2'
''
'END OF LESSON 4. TYPE QUIZ 4 TO CHECK, OR NEXT TO GO ON.'
∇
∇∆Q4;∆S;∆A
∆S←0
'WHAT IS 1 2 3>2?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+0 0 1 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS 1∧0?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+0 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS ~1 0 1?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+0 1 0 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS 3≠3?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+0 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS (1 2 3>1)×10 20 30?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+0 20 30 ∆CHECK ∆A
'YOU GOT ';∆S;' OF 5.'
∇
∇∆L5 ∆F;∆Q
→(S1,S2,S3,S4)[1⌈4⌊∆F]
S1:'LESSON 5: REDUCTION AND SCAN'
''
'F/V PUTS THE FUNCTION F BETWEEN THE ELEMENTS OF V. +/ IS'
'THE SUM, ×/ THE PRODUCT, ⌈/ THE LARGEST, ⌊/ THE SMALLEST.'
'TRY:'
''
'      +/1 2 3 4'
'      ×/2 3 4'
'      ⌈/3 9 2'
''
'THIS IS REDUCTION, AND / IS AN OPERATOR: IT TAKES A'
'FUNCTION AND MAKES A NEW ONE. -/1 2 3 IS 1-2-3 READ FROM'
'THE RIGHT, 1-(2-3), WHICH IS 2.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S2:'ON 1 AND 0, ∧/ ASKS WHETHER ALL ARE 1 AND ∨/ WHETHER ANY'
'IS. +/ COUNTS THEM. TRY:'
''
'      ∧/1 1 0'
'      ∨/1 2 3>2'
'      +/2 4 6 8>3'
''
'THE LAST COUNTS HOW MANY ELEMENTS EXCEED 3. NO LOOP WAS'
'WRITTEN: THE COMPARISON MADE A VECTOR OF 1S AND 0S, AND'
'THE REDUCTION SUMMED IT.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S3:'F\V IS SCAN: EVERY REDUCTION ALONG THE WAY. +\ IS THE'
'RUNNING TOTAL. TRY:'
''
'      +\1 2 3 4'
'      ×\1 2 3 4'
'      ⌈\3 1 4 1 5'
''
'×\⍳N IS THE FACTORIALS, AND ⌈\ IS THE LARGEST SO FAR.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S4:'THE AVERAGE OF A VECTOR IS ITS SUM OVER ITS LENGTH:'
''
'      (+/2 4 6)÷⍴2 4 6'
''
'REDUCING AN EMPTY VECTOR GIVES THE VALUE THAT LEAVES THE'
'FUNCTION ALONE: +/⍳0 IS 0 AND ×/⍳0 IS 1. SO THE SUM OF'
'NOTHING IS 0, AS IT SHOULD BE, AND NO CASE IS SPECIAL.'
''
'END OF LESSON 5. TYPE QUIZ 5 TO CHECK, OR NEXT TO GO ON.'
∇
∇∆Q5;∆S;∆A
∆S←0
'WHAT IS +/1 2 3 4?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+10 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS ×/2 3 4?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+24 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS +\1 2 3?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+1 3 6 ∆CHECK ∆A
'WHAT IS ⌈/3 9 2?'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+9 ∆CHECK ∆A
'HOW MANY OF 2 4 6 8 EXCEED 3? TYPE THE NUMBER.'
∆A←∆READ
→('STOP' ∆IS ∆A)/0
∆S←∆S+3 ∆CHECK ∆A
'YOU GOT ';∆S;' OF 5.'
∇
∇∆L6 ∆F;∆Q
→(S1,S2,S3,S4)[1⌈4⌊∆F]
S1:'LESSON 6: SELECTION'
''
'BRACKETS AFTER A VECTOR PICK ELEMENTS BY POSITION, COUNTING'
'FROM 1. THE INDEX MAY BE A VECTOR TOO. TRY:'
''
'      V←10 20 30 40'
'      V[3]'
'      V[1 3]'
'      V[4 3 2 1]'
''
'THE ARROW, ←, GIVES V ITS VALUE, AND V KEEPS IT UNTIL YOU'
'CHANGE IT. A LINE THAT ONLY ASSIGNS PRINTS NOTHING. AN'
'INDEXED NAME ON THE LEFT OF THE ARROW CHANGES ONE ELEMENT:'
'V[2]←25, AND THEN V.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S2:'↑ TAKES THE FIRST N ELEMENTS AND ↓ DROPS THEM; A NEGATIVE'
'N COUNTS FROM THE END. TRY:'
''
'      2↑V'
'      ¯1↑V'
'      1↓V'
'      ¯2↓V'
''
'⌽ REVERSES, AND N⌽V ROTATES BY N: 1⌽V MOVES THE FIRST'
'ELEMENT TO THE END.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S3:'A VECTOR OF 1S AND 0S ON THE LEFT OF / SELECTS: THIS IS'
'COMPRESSION, AND WITH A COMPARISON IT IS HOW APL FILTERS.'
'TRY:'
''
'      (V>15)/V'
'      1 0 1 1/V'
'      1 0 1 1\1 2 3'
''
'THE LAST IS EXPANSION: THE 0 OPENS A GAP, FILLED WITH 0.'
∆TRY
∆Q←∆PAUSE
→∆Q/0
S4:'∊ ASKS WHETHER EACH ELEMENT ON THE LEFT IS SOMEWHERE ON'
'THE RIGHT, AND V⍳X ASKS WHERE: THE POSITION OF X IN V, OR'
'ONE PAST THE END IF IT IS NOT THERE. TRY:'
''
'      30∊V'
'      V⍳30'
'      V⍳99'
''
'END OF LESSON 6. TYPE QUIZ 6 TO CHECK, OR NEXT TO GO ON.'
∇
∇∆Q6;∆S;∆A
∆S←0
